  <HaltConditions>
    | 🛑 ID | Trigger |
    |---|---|
    | `H_OPERATOR_REJECTS_SETUP` | Operator declines to set the project up. Without the required scripts no SDD branch can run — state that plainly and stop |
  </HaltConditions>

  <ExecutionPlan>
    <Step id="STEP_0_ASSESS">
      <Goal>State the honest readiness gap, straight from sdd-state.</Goal>
      <Action>
        Take everything from `sdd-state`'s `[READINESS]` block — `package.json` present/absent, each
        required script declared/not, `lint→gennady`, `check→read-only`, `gennady-installed` — plus the
        `missing` list (which now names `format(read-only)` and `lint(read-only)` separately from
        `check(read-only)` when a formatter/linter write switch leaks into that script's own chain).
        That tool is the single source of state (`AX_TOOL_INVOCATION`); do not re-derive it by hand.
        Tell the operator, in plain Russian (`AX_OPERATOR_LANGUAGE`), exactly what is present and what is
        missing. No narration, just the gap. Flow into the plan (no STOP).
      </Action>
    </Step>
    <Step id="STEP_1_PLAN">
      <Goal>Lay out the fix and get a go-ahead.</Goal>
      <Action>
        From the `missing` list, lay out what will be done: patch `package.json` (real command where
        the tool is already a dependency, else a TODO stub), wire `lint` to `gennady`, keep `format` /
        `lint` read-only (any formatter/linter write switch moves to `format:fix` / `lint:fix`, each of
        which MUST carry one), seed the optional `check` / `fix` wrappers, and install the missing
        dependencies / `gennady`. If the operator wants
        to lock a real tool now (test runner,
        formatter, type-checker) — a genuine NEW pick, not the stub path — run a research gate first:
        2–3 targeted WebSearch queries (is each candidate alive and current, how AI-agent-friendly it is
        this year — text-configurable, autofix, machine-readable diagnostics) before offering 2–3
        options with trade-offs (`AX_DIVERGE_BEFORE_RECOMMEND`); for each option, one line on why it's
        offered per the research (not recall) plus the source. If WebSearch is unavailable here, say so
        plainly — `предлагаю по памяти на <дата обучения модели>, ресёрч недоступен` — a named caveat,
        not silence. Otherwise stub it and defer the choice to the infra flow (no research needed for a
        stub). Present the plan + Approval Check (`AX_OPERATOR_AGREEMENT` — no edits before an explicit
        go-ahead). STOP.
      </Action>
    </Step>
    <Step id="STEP_2_APPLY">
      <Goal>Do everything the agent can — edits and installs.</Goal>
      <Action>
        With approval: create `package.json` if absent (name + `scripts`); add the seven exact bricks —
        real command where the tool exists, else the TODO stub (see Reference Data); `lint` always
        reaches `gennady`; `format` / `lint` stay read-only, while `format:fix` / `lint:fix` MUST each
        carry a real write switch (`--write` / `--fix` / `--autofix`) — a fixer that never mutates is
        a configuration error, flagged the same as a missing script; seed the two optional wrappers
        too (`check` = `npx gennady sdd-verify --profile full`, staying read-only itself; `fix` =
        `format:fix` → `lint:fix`); AND run the dependency installs the project needs (`npm i -D …`,
        gennady) — try them — the corporate proxy often blocks installs, but not always. Re-run
        `sdd-state`. Anything that failed goes to STEP_3.
      </Action>
    </Step>
    <Step id="STEP_3_REPORT_FAILURES">
      <Goal>Report any failed command and hand it to the operator — once, no loop.</Goal>
      <Action>
        For each command that FAILED in STEP_2 (typically an install the proxy 403'd), immediately tell
        the operator the EXACT command that did not work and the error — once, no retry, no
        environment-fixing. Hand it over to run themselves. Present the
        failed-command block and wait (`HALT_FORMAT`-style). STOP until the operator runs them and says
        done («готово / дальше»).
      </Action>
    </Step>
    <Step id="STEP_4_VERIFY_AND_CONTINUE">
      <Goal>Confirm ready, then return control.</Goal>
      <Action>
        WHEN `STEP_3_REPORT_FAILURES` ran (some command failed and the operator stepped in by hand) →
        re-run `sdd-state` now — the operator's own actions changed state since STEP_2's snapshot, so
        it is stale. WHEN `STEP_3` was skipped (STEP_2 applied cleanly, nothing failed) → reuse
        STEP_2's own closing `sdd-state` re-run directly; it is already the fresh post-apply
        snapshot, and a second call here would just re-confirm it — a wasted turn. Either way:
        `READINESS=ready` → setup is done; return control to the caller (the router's classify/route
        step, or the skill's own flow). Still `not-ready` → state the remaining gap and resume at
        `STEP_1` for the leftover items only — a fresh assessment, never a blind re-run of a command
        that already failed.
      </Action>
    </Step>
  </ExecutionPlan>

  <ReferenceData>
    The seven exact bricks `sdd-state` checks (exact match — `type-check` also accepts the
    `typecheck` spelling, every other name has exactly one):

    | script | purpose | stubbable until infra flow? |
    |---|---|---|
    | `type-check` | type-check, no emit | yes |
    | `test` | run tests | yes |
    | `test:coverage` | tests with coverage | yes |
    | `format` | formatter in check-only mode — read-only | yes |
    | `format:fix` | formatter write mode — MUST carry `--write` / `--fix` / `--autofix` | yes (stub still needs the literal switch — see below) |
    | `lint` | lint chain that reaches `gennady` (DbC), read-only | **no — must wire gennady** |
    | `lint:fix` | linter autofix — MUST carry `--write` / `--fix` / `--autofix` | yes (same caveat) |

    `format` and `lint` — and every script either reaches via `npm run <x>` — must stay free of known
    write switches (`eslint … --fix`, `prettier … --write`, `… --autofix`); a leaked write switch
    fails that script's own read-only check (`format(read-only)` / `lint(read-only)` in `missing`),
    even if every script name is present. `format:fix` and `lint:fix` invert the rule: each MUST
    carry a real write switch — absent, it reports `format:fix(no --write/--fix/--autofix — a fixer
    that never mutates)` / `lint:fix(…)` in `missing`, exactly like a missing script. `check` and
    `fix` sit OUTSIDE the seven and never gate readiness — seed them anyway as the optional human/CI
    wrappers: `"check": "npx gennady sdd-verify --profile full"` (must itself stay read-only —
    `check(read-only)` in `missing` otherwise), `"fix": "npm run format:fix && npm run lint:fix"`.

    A minimal `package.json` that reads as **`provisional`** once gennady is installed — real `lint`,
    the rest stubbed until the infra flow locks real tools (point `lint` at the project's own source
    dirs). `provisional` is a real, named state, not a lesser `ready`: the seven bricks are in place,
    so bootstrap/config/doc phases and `scaffold` may proceed — but `sdd-task --phase` mechanically
    REFUSES every `impl`/`refactor`/`test` phase (`ERR_CLI_SDD_TASK_INFRA_NOT_READY`) and
    `sdd-verify --profile code|test|full` returns `⛔` for a stubbed foundation, because an `echo`
    that exits 0 verifies nothing and a green verdict on it would be a fiction. Only replacing every
    stub with a real tool moves the project to `ready` (= execution-ready).
    The `format:fix`/`lint:fix` stubs below carry a literal `--fix`/`--write` token as a placeholder
    switch — a plain `echo TODO` alone would report as a non-mutating fixer even before a real tool
    is picked:
    ```json
    {
      "scripts": {
        "type-check": "echo 'TODO: настроить инфраструктуру (type-check — tsc --noEmit)' >&2",
        "test": "echo 'TODO: настроить инфраструктуру (test runner)' >&2",
        "test:coverage": "echo 'TODO: настроить инфраструктуру (coverage)' >&2",
        "format": "echo 'TODO: настроить инфраструктуру (formatter, read-only check)' >&2",
        "format:fix": "echo 'TODO: настроить formatter --write (write mode)' >&2",
        "lint": "gennady lint src/",
        "lint:fix": "echo 'TODO: настроить linter --fix (autofix)' >&2",
        "check": "npx gennady sdd-verify --profile full",
        "fix": "npm run format:fix && npm run lint:fix"
      }
    }
    ```
    `lint` may reach `gennady` directly or through an `npm run <x>` chain — either satisfies the
    `lint→gennady` check. The leaf stubs print a warning to stderr but **exit 0 — by design they
    pass the SHAPE checks**; `format`/`lint`/`check` stay read-only-clean since neither their own
    body nor anything they reach carries a write switch, while `format:fix`/`lint:fix` pass the
    mutating check on their placeholder token alone. That is what makes this package.json
    `provisional` rather than `ready`: shape is satisfied, substance is not. `sdd-state` names every
    stub it found on the READINESS line, and that list IS the infra flow's worklist — the project
    reaches `ready` exactly when the list is empty.
  </ReferenceData>

